nt situation for some  context opening my  third notebook so  for this i'm creating here a small dummy  data frame uh with columns of  using different data types so i have a  data frame with  some integer data before data boolean  stream and timestamps um  if i look at the data types  um there are a few things that you can  notice  um so first thing and something probably  most of you will be  aware of that if you have integer data  but also missing values that it gets  caused to  two float data types so you can't store  integer data with missing values  currently in pandas  that's for integer data but also for  boolean data it can  natively store missing data so here  for missing data for boolean data it  gets  cast to object t type  in addition to this variable support  across data types  you can also notice that there are  different  indicators for the missing data that is  being used so for those two columns you  see here  none so it stands for not a number and  it's a floating point  value for the boolean and string data  we're actually using non here but it  could also be  none and in the time some data  then there is yet another indicator  nat for time  um so and this is  a quite yeah um i would say  a bit of messy situation uh not all data  types support it  different indicators so that's uh the  reason  why we're trying to improve um this  situation therefore  we are working on new what we call  nullable d types and  the 1.0 release  of this year introduced a new  indicator and the pd.na missing value  indicator so to replace  np.nan and non or nat as a missing value  sentinel  and it also introduced a few data types  that are already  started using this so we have a nullable  interior boolean and stream  data type in 1.0 in the upcoming  pandas 1.2 release to also be supported  for  floating data  [Music]  let's again look at the  previous data frame but now converted to  the new data types so the convert d  types  method is a small helper method to  convert  the columns of the data frame to  same data but using the new  data types so if you look at this  column so the the first thing you can  notice here  that now um all of those columns are  using the same  indicator for having missing data  the timestamp column not yet so that's  something we still have to do  uh in the future but for those columns  it's already this bd dot name  so that's a new  uh a new object that was introduced in  panels 1.0  and so it's used as the  indicator for uh missing data  it stands for not available it's the  same terminology as  i used in r for example and it's similar  to the null  from sql  [Music]  then if you look at the data types of my  data frame here  you can now see that it's my  column with integers is now actually  indicating that it's using it's an  integer data type  the same for my boolean column it's now  indicated it are actually  rules and not and this object d type  um and it's also using the the string  data type so the  um the string data type that i  introduced in the previous notebook  is also using this pd.net as their  as its missing value indicator  one note here you will see here the uh  the n64 with the capital so  that's a bit uh a tricky uh thing but to  differentiate from the numpy id type  which doesn't support missing values for  integers  we are using here the form we take  capital  um so for those data types where  to otherwise overlap with a  d type  so in general those  new nullable d types if you have columns  for those d types  using pd dot n a it would work  similarly as before if you used  uh none for flow data or or with object  t type  so all the functions specific to  missing data will work as expected  with it so things like fill in a isn't a  not an a  drop in a those functions they will all  work with  those noble d types as well  there are however a few differences in  behavior  that are worth noting compared to  the the current behavior when using non  or  none as missing value indicat